---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Image credits">
  <section class="container simple-page">
    <p class="label">Image credits</p>
    <h1>Image credits</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The Talosaur team edited the photos, graphics and the homepage video on this site from the sources below.</p>
      <p>Page headers, big photo bands, inset photos and Home page photos: video and photos from Pexels contributors (free to use under the Pexels license), including Joerg Mangelsen (ocean sunfish), ekamelev (bubble anemone) and Kindel Media (fish eye), and NOAA Ocean Exploration (public domain). The nature photos have false-colour edits.</p>
      <p>Page header photos on Who we are, News, Work with us and Positions (zoanthids, porcelain crab, soft coral, pygmy seahorse): [Photographer and source for these four photos.] Shown inverted (as negatives), some only in part.</p>
      <p>Page header photo on The Problem (jellyfish, supplied by the team): [photographer and source], with colour edits.</p>
      <p>Animals in the depth graphic on The Problem page: Tracey Jennings / Ocean Image Bank (squid), Gregory Piper / Ocean Image Bank (jellyfish), Mike Bartick / Ocean Image Bank (larval fish) and Richard Barnden / Ocean Image Bank (two drifting animals), used for non-commercial purposes. The squid and jellyfish have colour edits. The research ship there is our own drawing.</p>
      <p>Turtle engraving in the homepage video: David Bushnell's Turtle submarine (1775), public domain. The green Turtle drawing on the Home page is the team's own.</p>
      <p>Colour-edited accents: the radiolarian on the Home page is from Ernst Haeckel's Radiolaria plates for the HMS Challenger report (1887), public domain. The seafloor texture behind Latest news on the Home page is a NOAA Ocean Exploration multibeam sonar point cloud (Deepwater Wonders of Wake, 2016), and the small photo over the Donate closing photo (a tube anemone) is from NOAA Ocean Exploration dive video (Mona Canyon, 2018), public domain. The jellyfish on the Work with us page is from a Pexels video (Pexels license).</p>
      <p>Photos in the content sections (At a glance on The Problem, Who we work with, Where it goes): NOAA Ocean Exploration dive video (public domain) and Pexels videos (Pexels license). The maps on The Problem and News pages, and the dotted globe on The Problem and Home, are drawn from GSHHG coastline data (Wessel and Smith) and basemap's state boundaries.</p>
      <p>Full-width colour bands (Home, Who we are, Positions, Donate, Work with us): NOAA Ocean Exploration dive video (spiral coral, American Samoa, 2026; ray, expedition EX2206, 2022; feather star, Mona Canyon, 2018; public domain) and Pexels videos (sun rays, garden eels, zoanthids; Pexels license), all with colour edits. The thin strip of schooling fish on the Home page is from a Pexels video (Pexels license), with colour edits.</p>
      <p>Flower Garden Banks photos ("Who lives here" on The Problem, the Home "Get in touch" card and the Offshore stop on News), from NOAA's <a href="https://sanctuaries.noaa.gov/media/posters/2023-flower-garden-banks-national-marine-sanctuary-poster.pdf">2023 Flower Garden Banks National Marine Sanctuary poster</a>: G.P. Schmahl/NOAA (fish in reef, coral reef, marbled grouper, manta ray), Emma Hickerson/NOAA (golden smooth trunkfish) and NOAA/UNCW-UVP (black coral). The seafloor relief behind the Flower Garden Banks numbers is from the same poster, with colour edits.</p>
      <p>Drawing of Flower Garden Banks on The Problem page: the front of the same NOAA poster, cropped. Primary artist Kathryn Robertson; secondary artist and art direction Matt McIntosh/NOAA; supporting artist Melissa Cook.</p>
      <!-- PLACEHOLDER: who took the six photos the team sent for the plans sections, and where they are from -->
      <p>Photos in the plans sections on Who we are and Work with us (coral reef from the air, egrets over lily pads, glacier front, whale in sea ice, dolphins, reef wall), supplied by the team: [photographer and source for each]. The reef from the air and the lily pads behind "A small crew, for now" have edits.</p>
      <!-- PLACEHOLDER: who took the brain coral photo, and where it is from -->
      <p>Brain coral at Flower Garden Banks (The Problem, Habitat): [photographer and source].</p>
      <p>Homepage video: footage from Pexels (Pexels license) and NOAA Ocean Exploration (public domain), with these stills: deep-sea frames from MBARI (Monterey Bay Aquarium Research Institute), via FathomNet (CC BY-NC-ND 4.0); animals on black from Ocean Image Bank (The Ocean Agency), photographers Gregory Piper, Liang Fu, Mike Bartick, Richard Barnden and Tracey Jennings; specimen photos that appear to come from the Ocean Census; and public-domain plates and maps: Ernst Haeckel's radiolarians (1887 and 1904), a black bass skeleton (1900), Athanasius Kircher's map of Atlantis (1678) and NOAA's sonar map of a seamount. The octopus eye is by Tim Ritenour.</p>
      <!-- PLACEHOLDER: the stills in the homepage video whose source isn't known yet (see the media inventory) -->
      <p>[Sources still to find for some homepage video stills: six eye close-ups, the 1934 Loch Ness photograph, a Mosasaurus skeleton, a plesiosaur painting, a zooplankton painting, a map of the Mariana arc, a drawing of daily vertical migration, a sonar screenshot, a radiolarian drawing, a clipper ship painting and a mounted fish skeleton.]</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .simple-page {
    padding-block: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  h1 {
    margin-block: var(--space-sm) var(--space-lg);
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: 60ch;
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .prose p {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
</style>
